WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.860
Film Courage: When do you start thinking about
the structure of the story, key beats, turning

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:04.860
points?

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.890
Shane Stanley, Author/Filmmaker/Instructor:
I think you need to keep a reader engaged.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:16.309
I don’t think there is a set time but if
you are 15, 18, 20, 25, 30 pages in and we

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:23.109
still have no idea what is going on or there
hasn’t been that moment I kind of think

00:00:23.109 --> 00:00:27.539
you need to rethink some things.

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:31.720
There is the old saying that when you watch
a movie it’s that 42 to 46 minute point

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.660
that usually becomes a slow burn no matter
what the movie is.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.790
So I know a lot of writers and filmmakers
try to make sure that point of the film doesn’t

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:43.830
lack spark or an interest because that’s
a lot of times where people…they are not

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.730
going to give up on the film but they tend
to get a little restless.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.430
It seems a lot of people take a really long
time to get to the point and I’ve learned

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:58.190
that as an independent filmmaker when you
sell your material it’s more about we have

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:02.480
to remember this is a business and now in
days with phones and tablets and distractions

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:09.710
and pets and kids and everything else under
the sun going on we are constantly interrupted.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:14.520
Audiences when they see your film unless they
are really at a movie theater and truly turn

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.960
their phones off are never really 100% immersed
in your story, there is always something,

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.820
there is a noise outside, there is a cat knocking
over a vase, it’s a kid who is crying because

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:31.500
he didn’t get his way and he is hungry and
you have to realize that as a filmmaker what

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:38.900
you are up against and I think to just kind
of lull along to tell your story it’s very

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.369
rare that you are going to be able to compete
with anything.

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:45.569
So I think to be able to throw some twists
and turns to engage your audience into some

00:01:45.569 --> 00:01:49.990
new layers or elements or characters or what
is the point of this movie a little sooner

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:54.729
than we are used to I think needs to happen
more often and I a lot of writers are starting

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:55.729
to figure that out.

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:00.229
Film Courage: So then you wouldn’t be working
some of this out in the treatment stage?

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:02.899
It’s once you start the actual screenwriting?

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:08.060
Shane: I’ve gotten to the point now when
I work with writers that I tell them it’s

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:13.810
sadly there is kind of an 11-minute rule abroad
that we kind of need to know what this is

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.599
about, what’s going on and what we are signing
up for in the first 11 minutes of the actual

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:21.129
movie which is going to tell somebody what’s
going to happen in the first 12 to 15 pages

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:25.849
of the script and I don’t like telling stories
that way but I’ve also learned the hard

00:02:25.849 --> 00:02:26.849
way.

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:31.230
I’ve had some projects that you know hey
if you do this it will sell.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:37.569
Hey if you change this it will move and what
I’ve learned is the faster we get to the

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:41.769
point in storytelling and I know this goes
against everything from telling great stories

00:02:41.769 --> 00:02:47.879
of the old days but we are living in a new
time and it has really gotten to be about

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:50.439
get to the frickin’ point.

00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:52.260
What am I signing up for early?

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.799
They want to know, it’s like I’ll give
you an example in the film that I just is

00:02:55.799 --> 00:03:01.129
coming out BREAK EVEN it’s about four kids
who find 50 million dollars while scuba diving

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:07.140
at a remote island and we developed this opening
and this whole thing and this backstory and

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.239
why are these kids here and who are they and
what is their relationships?

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:16.459
And it was basically Yeah…we need to find
the 50 million dollars by 12 minutes in.

00:03:16.459 --> 00:03:19.209
It’s like wait a minute, that doesn’t
come in until page 30.

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:22.180
Well it needs to come in at about 12 minutes
in.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.050
So you have to restructure some things, you
have to cut scenes, you have to cut backstory

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:31.689
because at the end of the day when you are
selling a movie around the world they are

00:03:31.689 --> 00:03:34.790
signing up for something and if you’re selling
an action film, if you’re selling a thriller,

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:39.609
if you’re selling a suspense story, you
better get to it because there is too much

00:03:39.609 --> 00:03:43.500
competition, there are too many things that
are moving fast that will hold attention because

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:44.519
it moves so fast.

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:49.090
That’s where that MTV generation of cutting
came from is we don’t know how to tell a

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:52.430
good story so we are going to cut a bunch
of stuff really fast so you are afraid to

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:58.060
take your eyes off of the screen and that
has now found its way I think its found a

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.400
balance and storytelling with films a little
better but we need to get to the point and

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.209
if you look at the older films most of the
ones that I grew up with you know CHARIOTS

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:13.799
OF FIRE, THE BLACK STALLION, ENCHANTING APRIL
you are look at like 25, 28, 35, minutes in

00:04:13.799 --> 00:04:15.560
until you really kind of have an idea.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:21.959
If you watch the BLACK STALLION they don’t
introduce Mickey Rooney’s character until

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:23.729
about 48 minutes in.

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:27.900
The kids alone at the island for 30 minutes
for crying out loud and then he gets home

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.449
and he’s got to reintegrate in school and
then all of the sudden the horse runs away

00:04:30.449 --> 00:04:34.289
and then he goes on this trip to try and find
the horse and then there is your co-star.

00:04:34.289 --> 00:04:37.479
It was also that way with ON GOLDEN POND.

00:04:37.479 --> 00:04:44.160
They don’t bring Jane Fond in until 25 minutes
in so I don’t think those play as much any

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:49.290
more, not saying the films don’t hold up
as great cinema but we want to meet the players,

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.060
we want to know that the game is a lot quicker
now.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.061
Film Courage: Or even with The Stepford Wives
[1975 version] the original one.

00:04:56.061 --> 00:04:57.061
Shane: Well the original one.

00:04:57.061 --> 00:05:03.180
Film Courage: Right, long driving scenes with
the soundtrack and there was something comforting

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.180
about that.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:05.449
I don’t know if it would work today?

00:05:05.449 --> 00:05:08.100
Shane: Well Quentin [Tarantino] did it in
ONCE UPON A TIME IN HOLLYWOOD.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.100
I think he really exploited the driving scenes
but we’re looking at the back of Brad Pitt’s

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.350
and Leonardo DiCaprio’s heads so that makes
it a little different.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:19.280
But what I try to tell the independent filmmakers
if you’re not getting Brad or Leo as your

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.470
stars and I don’t even get them as my stars,
your international buyers want to get to the

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:25.470
point.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:30.500
And we are at a point now where we are able
to lock picture and we don’t make different

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.220
cuts for different markets or parts of the
globe.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:36.410
We have kind of learned what works comfortable
everywhere.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:39.430
You know there are 54 territories that we
sell to around the globe.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:47.800
What is it 170 countries and in that you have
54 territories and you want to try to do something

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:51.030
that is going to appeal to everybody and it’s
hard to please all the people all of the time

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.380
but getting to the point quickly is one that
you can really help sell that movie a lot

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:59.349
better if people know what they are signing
up for in the first 15, 20 minutes tops.

00:05:59.349 --> 00:06:04.360
Film Courage: Well that’s interesting because
people seem to get upset when we say or we

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:09.400
interview people that say if a script isn’t
good by 10 pages in, most people will stop

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:10.639
reading it.

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:14.190
And so then the comments are “You’re not
giving it a chance.”

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:16.440
Shane: Right.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:17.949
People just don’t have the patience anymore.

00:06:17.949 --> 00:06:20.870
You know again I hate to revert to it but
you go back to look at CLOSE ENCOUNTERS OF

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.430
THE THIRD KIND.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:25.199
Brilliant film…that is a slow burn.

00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:28.900
I think if that movie was made today that
would be a lot different.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:32.630
Not taking anything away from Steven Spielberg
who did a great job writing the script.

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:38.040
It was based on an experience he had as a
kid and a great job directing it but you’re

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:42.570
going to tell me that that movie today would
play at that pace, I don’t think so.

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:45.069
And you’re right a lot of people are “Well
you’re not giving it a chance.”

00:06:45.069 --> 00:06:48.289
And most readers will say if he doesn’t
get me by page eight I am done.

00:06:48.289 --> 00:06:52.470
I think you kind of have to know the executives
you know there was a trend where all of the

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:55.500
executives are saying to you you’ve got
to find that hook, it’s got to be edgy.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.300
I want it to be edgy and grab me.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.250
Shake me by my lapels by page eight!

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:04.840
So everybody came up with the edgy, crazy
opening that just shocked everybody.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:08.139
I don’t think shock and awe is what makes
people watch a whole movie.

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:12.030
People want to be drawn in, they want to care
about characters, they want to go along for

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:15.419
a journey and they know what they’re signing
up for but you have to make that journey interesting,

00:07:15.419 --> 00:07:18.570
you want to put some bends in the road, you
want to have some interesting things that

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:23.150
they are going to pass and I think you need
to occasionally give them a flat tire, throw

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:27.509
a rod in the engine, they are going to have
to get out, grab their bags, hitchhike, find

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:29.190
a ride.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.060
I think that is just where we are now.

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:33.710
People are just so bored I mean you look at
Twitter.

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:35.180
Twitter has an auto feed now.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:39.199
I can’t even go on it anymore because the
second I try to read what somebody tweeted

00:07:39.199 --> 00:07:44.270
it automatically scrolls and I can’t find
the tweet I was reading.

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:45.810
There is no way to stop that.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:52.340
That is how we are getting our information
now and we’ve got to find a balance in storytelling

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:56.580
but that is kind of what is working right
now and it’s frustrating.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:58.481
It’s a hard pace to keep up with.

00:07:58.481 --> 00:07:58.981
Question For The Viewers: What is your reaction
to the 11-minute rule, do you agree?

